    "snippet": "---\ntitle: The Biggest Celebrity Weddings From the Past 90 Years\nauthor: Lilly Blomquist\ndate: 2024-12-04\n---\nOver the course of the last 90 years, the world has witnessed countless celebrity weddings. Some of these A-list pairs wed in lavish affairs where they were decked out in designer looks, were surrounded by opulent d\u00e9cor, and celebrated with star-studded guest lists, which later graced the pages of tabloids and eventually websites. Other famous duos attempted to dodge the paparazzi, discreetly exchanging vows in secret ceremonies.\n\nRegardless of the nature of these celebrations, celebrity weddings tend to have one thing in common: One the public knows about them, they turn into cultural phenomenons. These high-profile affairs often set the trends in the industry and serve as a source of inspiration for to-be-weds. Even those who aren't actively planning a wedding can live vicariously through celebrity nuptials\u2014particularly ones that worked with a seemingly unlimited budget\u2014and often feel a sense of pride and connection when their favorite actor or singer gets married.\n\nAcross the past nine decades, from 1934 to 2024, once word gets out that an A-list couple has said \"I do,\" these celebrations quickly dominate conversations and headlines. Ever since social media was born, many members of the public have taken to these online platforms to voice their opinions on the celebration, allowing others to join the conversation. With the vast reach of image-sharing platforms, footage from the event\u2014both leaked and published\u2014tend to go viral, spreading across the internet like wildfire.\n\nIn honor of BRIDES' 90th anniversary, we're taking a look back at the biggest celebrity weddings from 1934 until 2024. From Grace Kelly and Prince Rainier of Monaco's elaborate soir\u00e9e to Beyonc\u00e9 and Jay-Z's intimate penthouse affair, here are the most talked-about, influential nuptials by year.\n\n1934: Princess Marina and Prince George\n\nIn 1934, the same year that the first issue of Brides hit the stands, the wedding of Princess Marina, Duchess of Kent, and Prince George, Duke of Kent, was the most highly anticipated and prominent event of the year. The two royals wed on November 29 in an Anglican ceremony at Westminster Abbey (which attracted huge crowds), followed by a private Greek Orthodox service in Buckingham Palace. Like many royal weddings to come, it featured a carriage procession and iconic fashion, particularly the bride's Edward Moly"
