    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Gutzon Borglum - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2003-11-09\n---\nname: Gutzon Borglum\nbirth_name: John Gutzon de la Mothe Borglum\nbirth_date: 25 March 1867\nbirth_place: St. Charles, Idaho Territory, U.S.\ndeath_date: 6 March 1941\ndeath_place: Chicago, Illinois, U.S.\nresting_place: Forest Lawn Memorial Park, Glendale, California\nnotable_works: Mount RushmoreStone Mountain\nfield: Sculpture, painting\ntraining: Mark HopkinsInstitute of ArtHarte Bret August 31, 2017 The Overland Monthly Samuel Carson Google BooksGutzon Borglum live January 7, 2021 Marriott Library \u2013 The University of UtahAcad\u00e9mie JulianDimmick Lauretta American Sculpture in the Metropolitan Museum of Art: A catalogue of works by artists born between 1865 and 1885 Hassler Donna J. August 31, 1999 Metropolitan Museum of Art 978-0-87099-923-9 Google Books\u00c9cole des Beaux-ArtsCalifornia School of DesignJohn Borglum live January 7, 2021 Galerie Gabrie\nmovement: Bull Moose PartyCullinane Michael Patrick 2017-12-11 Theodore Roosevelt's Ghost: The History and Memory of an American Icon 2024-05-06 LSU Press 978-0-8071-6673-4 en\nspouse: Mary Williams (m. May 20, 1909)\nchildren: 3, including Lincoln\n\nJohn Gutzon de la Mothe Borglum (March 25, 1867 \u2013 March 6, 1941) was an American sculptor best known for his work on Mount Rushmore. He is also associated with various other public works of art across the U.S., including Stone Mountain in Georgia, statues of Union General Philip Sheridan in Washington D.C. and in Chicago, as well as a bust of Abraham Lincoln exhibited in the White House by Theodore Roosevelt and now held in the United States Capitol crypt in Washington, D.C.Howard Shaff and Audrey Karl Shaff, Six Wars at a Time; The Life and Times of Gutzon Borglum, sculptor of Mount Rushmore, Center for Western Studies, St. Augustana College, Sioux Falls, South Dakota 1985, p"
