    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Our Favorite Movies Featuring the Beauty of Adoption\nauthor: Author Audrey Levens\ndate: 2025-01-01\n---\nAt Save the Storks, we believe adoption is a beautiful journey that brings people together in unexpected ways. Many movies do a wonderful job of highlighting this unique bond that comes from choosing adoption. From heartfelt stories of family to uplifting tales of courage, here are some of our favorite movies that showcase the beauty of adoption. Whether you're looking for a comedy, a classic, or a drama, these films remind us of the power of choosing adoption.\n\nElf (2003)\n\nJust in time to watch for the holidays! In this beloved Christmas movie, Buddy the Elf (Will Ferrell) learns that he's adopted after growing up at the North Pole. He travels to New York City to meet his biological father, sparking a heartwarming and hilarious journey. Elf beautifully highlights the theme of chosen family and the unique love that adoptive families share.\n\nLes Mis\u00e9rables (2012)\n\nIn 19th-century France, Les Mis\u00e9rables follows Jean Valjean (Hugh Jackman), who adopts young Cosette after her mother's tragic death. This epic musical portrays themes of redemption, sacrifice, and love, making it one of the most moving films about adoption and family.\n\nContent Warning: Mature themes, violence, and intense scenes.\n\nJuno (2007)\n\nIn Juno, a teenage girl faces an unexpected pregnancy and bravely chooses to place her baby for adoption with a couple she carefully selects. One of the most life-affirming moments in the film occurs when Juno's friend reminds her that babies already have fingernails in the womb. This small but powerful detail emphasizes the humanity of the unborn. While Juno is a secular film and the main character's journey isn't perfect, its themes are strikingly life-affirming, highlighting the courage and selflessness it takes to choose adoption.\n\nContent Warning: Mature themes.\n\nLion (2016)\n\nBased on a true story, Lion follows Saroo, who is separated from his family as a young child and later adopted by an Australian couple. As an adult, Saroo uses Google Earth to trace his roots and reunite with his birth family. This emotionally powerful film highlights the beauty of adoption and the universal search for belonging.\n\nContent Warning: Child endangerment and intense themes.\n\nInstant Family (2018)\n\nInstant Family is an inspiring comedy-drama about a couple who decide to adopt three siblings from foster care. Through laughter,"
